Subject: Time-symmetric Jolly-Seber models with missing values - possible?
Message-ID: <5pI90c161y4.y6rjQ60yAwAJ@hmecology.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/hmecology/c/5pI90c161y4/m/y6rjQ60yAwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear modellers!

I have Constant Effort mist netting data and I want to fit an
integrated model with pop. growth, recruitment and survival. I am just
looking into time-symmetric Jolly-Seber models. In Marc & Michaels
awesome book BPA it is chapter 10.3.1 and 10.3.3 (basically, these
models are the same or similar to Time-symmetric open models used in
MARK - see MARK book chapter 13). These models use the capture history
to estimate both recruitment and survival (and pop. growth) at the
same time.

The problem arises when there are missing data in the capture history
(some sites were not active in some years, ie there are NAs in capture
history instead of zeroes). I did a simple experiment with the BPA
10.3.1 and 10.3.3 examples - in 2/3 of the sites I rewrote the first 5
occasions of the capture history to NA. The recruitment parameters and
the abundance were strongly biased in first 5 occasions (see the
results below).

This is almost certainly a result of the "strange" parametrization of
recruitment in the time-symmetric models. If we had only to compute
recruitment, we would just simply reverse the capture history and go
backwards, in similar way as in survival - no problem with missing
visits there. But when we compute BOTH survival and recruitment, in
bayesian model we cannot go back and forth at the same time.

So, now, having data with missing visits, and wanting to build an
integrated model - can this be done somehow in the time-symmetric
approach? Or do I have to just give up and duplicate the capture
histories, and compute survival on one copy of the data, and
recruitment on another (reading it from backwards) in the same
integrated model?

Thanks!

Tomas

